      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In the 1880s, the land that is now Canyon Country became part of Henry Newhall’s Newhall Ranch empire, later forming the Newhall Land and Farming Company.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canyon_Country%2C_Santa_Clarita%2C_California",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Buckweed Fire of October 2007 scorched nearly 38,000 acres across Canyon Country and neighboring Agua Dulce, highlighting the area’s wildfire risk.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canyon_Country%2C_Santa_Clarita%2C_California",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The historic Sand Canyon station, once a Southern Pacific rail stop, is listed as a California Historic Landmark within Canyon Country.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canyon_Country%2C_Santa_Clarita%2C_California",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Canyon Country’s development into a residential community accelerated in the 1960s and 1970s under the Newhall Land company’s suburban projects.",
